  v1: pH 5.75
  v1: not yet modelled — zinc stays as the metal: nothing dissolved here sits below it in the activity series. Its slow reaction with water itself — hydrogen over hours, a passivating hydroxide skin — is a rate this lab does not model
  v1: zinc does not corrode — nothing rusts in this vessel, because there is no oxygen in it. Corrosion needs three things at once — the metal, liquid water and oxygen — since it is a circuit: zinc would give up electrons at the anode, oxygen would take them at the cathode, and the water carries the ions between the two. This bench does not yet draw oxygen from the room into an open beaker, so the oxygen has to be put in the vessel for the slow clock to see it
